Trees :
Core concept

In inorder traversal, you always follow this order:

Left → Root → Right



     4
     / \
    2   5
   / \
  1   3

  Go left → 2
Go left → 1 → visit 1
Back to 2 → visit 2
Go right → 3 → visit 3
Back to 4 → visit 4
Go right → 5 → visit 5

Core difference (1-line each)
Inorder (L → Root → R) → process node in between children
Preorder (Root → L → R) → process node before children
Postorder (L → R → Root) → process node after children
When to use each
1. Inorder → Sorted / ordered processing

Use when:

You want elements in sorted order (in a BST)
You need infix expression (like normal math: a + b)

👉 Think: “Give me data in order”

2. Preorder → Copy / build / serialize tree

Use when:

You want to clone or serialize a tree
You need to process parent before children
Used in prefix expressions (+ a b)

👉 Think: “Start from root, then expand”

3. Postorder → Delete / bottom-up computation

Use when:

You need to delete/free a tree
You need results from children before parent
Used in evaluating expression trees

👉 Think: “Finish children first, then parent”

Quick comparison table
Traversal	Order	Use case	Key idea
Inorder	L → Root → R	BST sorted output	Middle processing
Preorder	Root → L → R	Tree copy / serialization	Top-down
Postorder	L → R → Root	Deletion / bottom-up problems	Bottom-up
Easy memory trick
Pre → root comes before
In → root comes in between
Post → root comes after

